Title: "Relations"
Id: "61ac6940-a021-4d54-bc78-910c75ce3a04"


Text: "A bare mathematical set is an unstructured collection of points,
       possessing neither geometry, hierarchy, nor internal dynamics. To breathe
       mathematical life into a set, one must specify how its elements interact.
       This is the fundamental role of a *relation*.

       Formally, a binary relation on a set $X$ is realized simply as a subset
       of the Cartesian product $X \times X$. Yet despite this austere
       set-theoretic definition, relations embody the primary structural motifs
       of all modern mathematics:
       - *Equivalence relations* model symmetry, grouping elements into clusters
         of indistinguishability that collapse to form quotient structures.
       - *Order relations* model hierarchy and precedence, organizing elements
         into partially or totally ordered posets and lattices.
       - *Functions*—the subject of the following chapter—are single-valued
         relations that govern dynamics and transformations."
Id: "c29d6027-fe93-4e6d-94df-f72a802fedcb"


SectionTitle: "Binary Relations and Their Properties"
Id: "c43de393-3ca6-474f-9c32-2996cb1b320b"


[\relation:?on{X}]
Declares: R \:subset:/ (.X \.set.cross./ X.)
when: X is \set
Enables:
. capability: R(x_, y_) :=> (x_, y_) "in"? R
  written: "x_? \: R \: y_?"
Documented:
. called: "relation on $X?$"
. description: "A relation on a set $X$ is a subset $R$ of $X \times X$. One
                writes $x \mathrel{R} y$ to mean that the pair $(x, y)$ belongs
                to $R$, so a relation is just a precise record of which ordered
                pairs are related."
Id: "5ace60a2-cc66-4cea-818a-538f877b5f97"


Text: "A relation $R$ on $X$ is *reflexive* when every element is related to
       itself: $\forall x \in X, x \mathrel{R} x$. In geometric terms, this
       means $R$ contains the diagonal $\Delta_X = \{(x, x) : x \in X\}$."
Id: "35a87f95-8d23-44a0-ae4f-70d4384a3db6"


[\(reflexive)::relation:?on{X}]
Refines: R
when: X is \set
satisfies:
. forAll: x "in" X
  then: R(x, x)
Documented:
. adjective: "reflexive"
. description: "A relation $R$ on $X$ is reflexive if $x \mathrel{R} x$ for all
                $x \in X$."
Id: "8db6967b-79de-4fd6-8769-fcd651c7c6ee"


Text: "A relation $R$ on $X$ is *symmetric* when the direction of relatedness is
       bidirectional: $x \mathrel{R} y \implies y \mathrel{R} x$. In relational
       algebra, symmetry is equivalent to the identity $R = R^{-1}$."
Id: "b2c8da90-8a30-4542-a3a3-f81bf989953a"


[\(symmetric)::relation:?on{X}]
Refines: R
when: X is \set
satisfies:
. forAll: x, y "in" X
  where: R(x, y)
  then: R(y, x)
Documented:
. adjective: "symmetric"
. description: "A relation $R$ on $X$ is symmetric if $x \mathrel{R} y$ implies
                $y \mathrel{R} x$."
Id: "273288f5-a313-4140-a28c-16ac7e8ba72b"


Text: "A relation $R$ on $X$ is *transitive* when connections bridge across
       intermediate nodes:
       $x \mathrel{R} y \land y \mathrel{R} z \implies x \mathrel{R} z$.
       Transitivity is the algebraic guarantee that local relationships cohere
       into global reachability."
Id: "5965ba24-25b8-4a16-92fc-035988cfb21c"


[\(transitive)::relation:?on{X}]
Refines: R
when: X is \set
satisfies:
. forAll: x, y, z "in" X
  where:
  . R(x, y)
  . R(y, z)
  then: R(x, z)
Documented:
. adjective: "transitive"
. description: "A relation $R$ on $X$ is transitive if $x \mathrel{R} y$ and
                $y \mathrel{R} z$ imply $x \mathrel{R} z$."
Id: "8baba6bf-25f4-48b4-84f9-4b2db59ccf47"


Text: "A relation $R$ on $X$ is *antisymmetric* when cycles of length two can
       only occur trivially:
       $x \mathrel{R} y \land y \mathrel{R} x \implies x = y$. Antisymmetry
       prohibits mutual preference between distinct elements, serving as the
       defining non-symmetric property of orders."
Id: "bdbeccce-dfdb-4d54-8b0c-92cae82c56d7"


[\(antisymmetric)::relation:?on{X}]
Refines: R
when: X is \set
satisfies:
. forAll: x, y "in" X
  where:
  . R(x, y)
  . R(y, x)
  then: x = y
Documented:
. adjective: "antisymmetric"
. description: "A relation $R$ on $X$ is antisymmetric if $x \mathrel{R} y$ and
                $y \mathrel{R} x$ imply $x = y$."
Id: "8ed4e2b2-f1b7-4131-8ae2-2834e71805fa"


Text: "The *inverse relation* (or converse) $R^{-1}$ reverses the direction of
       all ordered pairs: $(y, x) \in R^{-1} \iff (x, y) \in R$."
Id: "ece9bae3-c088-459e-9cd1-7a650d9b3681"


[\relation.inverse:of{R}]
Defines: S \:subset:/ (.X \.set.cross./ X.)
using: X is \set
when: R is \relation:on{X}
expresses:
. let: x, y "in" X
  then:
  . have: S(x, y)
    iff: R(y, x)
Documented:
. called: "inverse relation of $R?$"
. written: "R+?^{-1}"
. description: "The inverse relation $R^{-1}$ consists of all pairs $(x, y)$
                such that $(y, x) \in R$."
Id: "b182f12e-f94f-40e7-b5b7-c84dd243c262"


SectionTitle: "Equivalence Relations and Quotients"
Id: "66f53348-5a61-4763-9645-64390784903b"


Text: "Among all binary relations, the most ubiquitous are *equivalence
       relations*. An equivalence relation is simultaneously reflexive,
       symmetric, and transitive. These three axioms distill the essential
       properties of equality itself, allowing mathematicians to treat distinct
       objects as 'the same' with respect to a specified invariant."
Id: "b906ce0b-34d4-4b24-b168-d6a8d05efc92"


[\equivalence.relation:on{X}]
Declares: R is \relation:on{X}
when: X is \set
satisfies:
. [reflexive]
  forAll: x "in" X
  then: R(x, x)
. [symmetric]
  forAll: x, y "in" X
  where: R(x, y)
  then: R(y, x)
. [transitive]
  forAll: x, y, z "in" X
  where:
  . R(x, y)
  . R(y, z)
  then: R(x, z)
Documented:
. called: "equivalence relation on $X?$"
. description: "An equivalence relation on $X$ is a relation that is reflexive
                ($x \mathrel{R} x$), symmetric (if $x \mathrel{R} y$ then $y
                \mathrel{R} x$), and transitive (if $x \mathrel{R} y$ and $y
                \mathrel{R} z$ then $x \mathrel{R} z$). Such a relation sorts the
                elements of $X$ into classes of mutually related elements."
Id: "2394a97b-5960-4919-98db-b95ec3870343"


Text: "Given an equivalence relation $R$ on $X$, each element $x \in X$
       generates its *equivalence class*:
       $$[x]_R := \{y \in X : x \mathrel{R} y\}$$ Every element belongs to its
       own equivalence class (by reflexivity), and two classes $[x]_R$ and
       $[y]_R$ are either strictly identical or completely disjoint."
Id: "308e4d46-7205-4977-9583-486771d4ca48"


[\equivalence.class:of{x}:over{R}]
Defines: C := \set@{y_ : y_ "in" X | R(x, y_)}
using: X is \set
when:
. x "in" X
. R is \equivalence.relation:on{X}
Documented:
. called: "equivalence class of $x?$ on $R?$"
. written: "\left [ x? \right ]_{R?}"
. description: "The equivalence class $[x]_R$ of an element $x$ is the set of
                all elements related to $x$ by $R$. Every element lies in
                exactly one class, and two elements share a class precisely when
                they are related."
Id: "2fae575c-13c9-498b-b14a-e471f0e3cfdc"


Text: "Gathering all equivalence classes into a new set yields the *quotient
       set*: $$X / R := \{[x]_R : x \in X\}$$ Passing to the quotient is
       mathematics' universal engine of abstraction: it forgets irrelevant
       distinguishing details, collapsing entire equivalence classes into single
       points. The canonical quotient projection $\pi : X \to X / R$, defined by
       $\pi(x) = [x]_R$, is the prototypical surjective homomorphism."
Id: "7435ef90-380d-4429-a369-37d652479b85"


[X \.mod./ R]
Defines: I := \set@{ \equivalence.class:of{x_}:over{R} : x_ "in" X }
when:
. X is \set
. R is \equivalence.relation:on{X}
Documented:
. called: "$X?$ mod $R?$"
. written: "X? / R?"
. description: "The quotient set $X / R$ is the set whose elements are the
                equivalence classes of $R$. It collapses each class to a single
                point, so that elements related by $R$ become equal in the
                quotient."
Id: "cea0d898-a18d-44dc-b04c-ffb2f0cde26e"


[\partition:of{X}]
Declares: P \:subset:/ \power.set:of{X}
when: X is \set
satisfies:
. \empty.set \.not.in./ P
. forAll: x "in" X
  then:
  . existsUnique: A is \set
    suchThat:
    . A "in"? P
    . x "in"? A
Documented:
. called: "partition of $X?$"
. description: "A partition is a family of nonempty subsets in which every
                element of $X$ lies in exactly one block."
Id: "4c2d728b-0359-432c-afc5-371028a71eba"


SectionTitle: "Order Relations"
Id: "88c4093d-566b-48bb-baea-9926345a182f"


Text: "While equivalence relations formalize symmetry and clustering, *order
       relations* formalize comparison, ranking, and hierarchy.

       A *partial order* is reflexive, antisymmetric, and transitive. It allows
       incomparable elements: two elements $x, y$ may satisfy neither $x \le y$
       nor $y \le x$ (as in subset inclusion $\mathcal{P}(U)$ or divisibility in
       $\mathbb{N}$). A *total order* (or linear order) is a partial order in
       which every pair of elements is comparable ($x \le y \lor y \le x$),
       eliminating all ambiguity of precedence."
Id: "b69a5d44-be1d-4f87-bab6-f5c4eccf221c"


[\partial.order:on{X}]
Declares: R is \relation:on{X}
when: X is \set
satisfies:
. [reflexive]
  forAll: x "in" X
  then: R(x, x)
. [antisymmetric]
  forAll: x, y "in" X
  where:
  . R(x, y)
  . R(y, x)
  then: x = y
. [transitive]
  forAll: x, y, z "in" X
  where:
  . R(x, y)
  . R(y, z)
  then: R(x, z)
Documented:
. called: "partial order on $X?$"
. description: "A partial order on $X$ is a reflexive, antisymmetric, and
                transitive relation."
Id: "656a5a0a-77e8-42ea-857c-4f7d8eda00ac"


[\total.order:on{X}]
Declares: R is \partial.order:on{X}
when: X is \set
satisfies:
. forAll: x, y "in" X
  then:
  . anyOf:
    . R(x, y)
    . R(y, x)
Documented:
. called: "total order on $X?$"
. description: "A total (or linear) order on $X$ is a partial order in which
                every two elements are comparable."
Id: "40d12e9b-449e-49b9-a292-9388147d3e69"


SectionTitle: "Closures and Order Structure"
Id: "fde224c5-920b-4246-8da5-19dc66c144a2"


[\reflexive.closure:of{R}:on{X}]
Defines: S is \(reflexive)::relation:?on{X}
when:
. X is \set
. R is \relation:on{X}
expresses:
. R \:subset?:/ S
. forAll: T is \(reflexive)::relation:?on{X}
  where: R \:subset?:/ T
  then: S \:subset?:/ T
Documented:
. called: "reflexive closure of $R?$ on $X?$"
. description: "The least reflexive relation on $X$ containing $R$."
Id: "38e1561b-dcdb-4e2e-8ba0-53ef665a0c33"


[\symmetric.closure:of{R}:on{X}]
Defines: S is \(symmetric)::relation:?on{X}
when:
. X is \set
. R is \relation:on{X}
expresses:
. R \:subset?:/ S
. forAll: T is \(symmetric)::relation:?on{X}
  where: R \:subset?:/ T
  then: S \:subset?:/ T
Documented:
. called: "symmetric closure of $R?$ on $X?$"
. description: "The least symmetric relation on $X$ containing $R$."
Id: "ce0a9702-4034-4063-a435-5eafa76158cf"


[\transitive.closure:of{R}:on{X}]
Defines: S is \(transitive)::relation:?on{X}
when:
. X is \set
. R is \relation:on{X}
expresses:
. R \:subset?:/ S
. forAll: T is \(transitive)::relation:?on{X}
  where: R \:subset?:/ T
  then: S \:subset?:/ T
Documented:
. called: "transitive closure of $R?$ on $X?$"
. description: "The least transitive relation on $X$ containing $R$."
Id: "bd748bdf-4d7a-442b-bf35-4724dd70775c"


[\reflexive.transitive.closure:of{R}:on{X}]
Defines: S is \(reflexive, transitive)::relation:?on{X}
when:
. X is \set
. R is \relation:on{X}
expresses:
. R \:subset?:/ S
. forAll: T is \(reflexive, transitive)::relation:?on{X}
  where: R \:subset?:/ T
  then: S \:subset?:/ T
Documented:
. called: "reflexive--transitive closure of $R?$ on $X?$"
. description: "The least reflexive and transitive relation on $X$ containing
                $R$."
Id: "374659d6-cfcc-4262-b98e-b50207d16c18"


Text: "In graph-theoretic terms, the reflexive--transitive closure $R^*$
       corresponds to reachability in a directed graph: $x \mathrel{R^*} y$ if
       and only if there exists a finite path
       $x = v_0 \mathrel{R} v_1 \mathrel{R} \dots \mathrel{R} v_k = y$ of length
       $k \ge 0$. Setting $k = 0$ provides reflexivity ($x = y$)."
Id: "5fd1260e-1250-4b35-a679-218b2f4c0459"


Theorem:
given:
. X is \set
. R is \equivalence.relation:on{X}
then: X \.mod./ R is? \partition:of{X}
Documented:
. called: "Equivalence Classes Form a Partition"
. description: "The quotient by an equivalence relation is the partition of the
                domain into its equivalence classes."
Proof: """Let $R$ be an equivalence relation on $X$. We verify the defining
          properties of a partition for the quotient $X / R$:
          1. *Nonemptiness of blocks*: For each $x \in X$, reflexivity gives
             $x \mathrel{R} x$, so $x \in [x]_R$. Thus no equivalence class is
             empty: $\emptyset \notin X / R$.
          2. *Exhaustive coverage*: Since $x \in [x]_R$ for every $x \in X$, the
             union of all equivalence classes covers $X$.
          3. *Pairwise disjointness*: Suppose $[x]_R \cap [y]_R \neq \emptyset$.
             Then there exists $z \in [x]_R \cap [y]_R$, meaning
             $x \mathrel{R} z$ and $y \mathrel{R} z$. By symmetry,
             $z \mathrel{R} y$. By transitivity,
             $x \mathrel{R} z \land z \mathrel{R} y \implies x \mathrel{R} y$.
             For any $w \in [y]_R$, $y \mathrel{R} w$; by transitivity again,
             $x \mathrel{R} y \land y \mathrel{R} w \implies x \mathrel{R} w$,
             so $w \in [x]_R$. Thus $[y]_R \subseteq [x]_R$. Symmetrically,
             $[x]_R \subseteq [y]_R$, proving $[x]_R = [y]_R$. Hence distinct
             equivalence classes are strictly disjoint, and every element
             belongs to a unique block."""
Id: "c6cf2b6a-9343-4865-b41e-183b454edd24"


Theorem:
given:
. X is \set
. P is \partition:of{X}
then:
. exists: R is \equivalence.relation:on{X}
  suchThat:
  . forAll: x, y "in" X
    then:
    . have: R(x, y)
      iff:
      . exists: A is \set
        suchThat:
        . A "in"? P
        . x "in"? A
        . y "in"? A
Documented:
. called: "A Partition Determines an Equivalence Relation"
. description: "Two elements are equivalent precisely when they belong to the
                same block of the partition."
Proof: """Let $P$ be a partition of $X$. Define a relation $R$ by
          $x \mathrel{R} y$ if and only if there exists a block $A \in P$ such
          that $x \in A$ and $y \in A$. We verify that $R$ is an equivalence
          relation:
          1. *Reflexivity*: Because $P$ partitions $X$, every $x \in X$ belongs
             to some block $A \in P$. Since $x \in A$ and $x \in A$, we have
             $x \mathrel{R} x$.
          2. *Symmetry*: If $x \mathrel{R} y$, there is some $A \in P$ with
             $x, y \in A$. Since $y, x \in A$, we have $y \mathrel{R} x$.
          3. *Transitivity*: If $x \mathrel{R} y$ and $y \mathrel{R} z$, there
             exist blocks $A, B \in P$ such that $x, y \in A$ and $y, z \in B$.
             Then $y \in A \cap B$, so $A \cap B \neq \emptyset$. Because
             distinct blocks of a partition are mutually disjoint, $A = B$. Thus
             $x, z \in A$, which proves $x \mathrel{R} z$. Hence $R$ is an
             equivalence relation on $X$."""
Id: "97d1309a-6739-4ff9-a354-f0c6859df60f"


[\is.maximal{m}:in{X}:under{R}]
States:
when:
. X is \set
. R is \partial.order:on{X}
. m "in" X
that:
. not:
  . exists: x "in" X
    suchThat:
    . m != x
    . R(m, x)
Documented:
. called: "$m?$ is maximal in $X?$ under $R?$"
. description: "A maximal element has no distinct element strictly above it. In
                a non-total poset, a set may possess multiple distinct maximal
                elements."
Id: "690ee3e8-39a6-4853-8df9-7d0b9e1cec5a"


[\is.greatest{g}:in{X}:under{R}]
States:
when:
. X is \set
. R is \partial.order:on{X}
. g "in" X
that:
. forAll: x "in" X
  then: R(x, g)
Documented:
. called: "$g?$ is the greatest element of $X?$ under $R?$"
. description: "The greatest element (maximum) exceeds or equals every element
                of $X$. If a greatest element exists, it is unique and is the
                unique maximal element."
Id: "8c3b1160-03ac-4f70-856f-8dbdff0cba12"


[\is.minimal{m}:in{X}:under{R}]
States:
when:
. X is \set
. R is \partial.order:on{X}
. m "in" X
that:
. not:
  . exists: x "in" X
    suchThat:
    . m != x
    . R(x, m)
Documented:
. called: "$m?$ is minimal in $X?$ under $R?$"
. description: "A minimal element has no distinct element strictly below it."
Id: "3c3478fa-e156-4364-9c53-3577a60d7dd9"


[\is.least{l}:in{X}:under{R}]
States:
when:
. X is \set
. R is \partial.order:on{X}
. l "in" X
that:
. forAll: x "in" X
  then: R(l, x)
Documented:
. called: "$l?$ is the least element of $X?$ under $R?$"
. description: "The least element (minimum) is smaller than or equal to every
                element of $X$. If a least element exists, it is unique and is
                the unique minimal element."
Id: "33141ee6-c2fe-4116-8eff-3b6064d5d4e3"


[\lattice]
Declares: L ::= (X, R, meet(a_, b_), join(a_, b_)) is \set via X
specifies:
. R is \partial.order:on{X}
. meet(a_, b_) is \function:on{X \.set.cross./ X}:to{X}
. join(a_, b_) is \function:on{X \.set.cross./ X}:to{X}
satisfies:
. forAll: a, b "in" X
  then:
  . R(meet(a, b), a)
  . R(meet(a, b), b)
  . R(a, join(a, b))
  . R(b, join(a, b))
. forAll:
  . a, b "in" X
  . x "in" X
  where:
  . R(x, a)
  . R(x, b)
  then: R(x, meet(a, b))
. forAll:
  . a, b "in" X
  . x "in" X
  where:
  . R(a, x)
  . R(b, x)
  then: R(join(a, b), x)
Documented:
. called: "lattice"
. description: "A lattice is a partially ordered set in which each pair has a
                greatest lower bound and a least upper bound."
Id: "1454c69c-e96e-4c4e-98e4-7736db0ef0a7"
